/* ============================================================================
complex-roots-of-unity.epher -- the five fifth roots of unity

The n-th roots of unity are e^(2 pi i k / n) = cos(2 pi k / n) + i sin(2
pi k / n) for k = 0 .. n - 1: they sit on the unit circle and their sum
is zero (Churchill and Brown, "Complex Variables and Applications",
sec. 9). A for loop collects the five roots of x^5 = 1; a quiet while
loop adds the real and imaginary parts separately (they cancel to about
1e-16, so the transcript prints the booleans, not the residues), and
raising the first nontrivial root to the fifth power returns 1.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/algebra/complex/complex-roots-of-unity.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\algebra\complex\complex-roots-of-unity.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/algebra/complex/complex-roots-of-unity.epher

No knobs to edit; the fifth roots below are fixed examples.
============================================================================ */

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
// the five fifth roots of 1, k = 0..4
for k in 0 to 4 do cos(2 * pi * k / 5) + i * sin(2 * pi * k / 5)
// their sums, accumulated quietly as separate real counters
s = {0, 0, 0}
while s[1] < 5 do s = {s[1] + 1, s[2] + cos(2 * pi * s[1] / 5), s[3] + sin(2 * pi * s[1] / 5)}
print("the roots sum to ~0:", abs(s[2]) < 1e-9 and abs(s[3]) < 1e-9)
print("first root to the 5th power is 1:", abs((cos(2 * pi / 5) + i * sin(2 * pi / 5)) ^ 5 - 1) < 1e-9)
/* ---- expected output ----
= {1, 0.309016994375+0.951056516295i, -0.809016994375+0.587785252292i, -0.809016994375-0.587785252292i, 0.309016994375-0.951056516295i}
= {0, 0, 0}
= the roots sum to ~0: true
= first root to the 5th power is 1: true
*/
